acter_summary": {"Character": "character_description"}, "character_creator": {"Character": "creator_name"}, "portrayed_by": {"Performer": "performer_name"}, "performer_bio": {"Performer": "performer_bio"}, "performer_gender": {"Performer": "gender"}, "franchise_or_work": {"Work": "work_title"}, "work_summary": {"Work": "work_description"}, "work_format": {"Work": "format"}, "work_genre": {"Work": "genre"}, "release_date": {"Work": "release_date"}}}, "reasoning": "Identified recurring real-world entities across all sources: (1) fictional Character, (2) Work/franchise where the character appears, and (3) Performer/actor who portrays the character. To be semantically correct and redundancy-free, performer demographic attributes (birth_date, birth_place, gender, bio) are stored only on Performer; work attributes (title, description, format/medium/type, genre, date) only on Work; and character attributes (name, description, creator) only on Character. Because a character can appear in many works and a work has many characters, a minimal bridge table CharacterAppearance captures the M:N relationship. Because portrayals are specific to a character within a given work and can vary by adaptation, a separate Portrayal entity links Character+Work+Performer. Source columns were mapped to the closest integrated attribute by meaning (e.g., type/work_medium/work_format -> Work.format; publication_date/first_published_at/release_date -> Work.release_date; actor/portrayed_by -> Performer.performer_name). Table 1 lacks creator, birthplace, gender, and genre; these remain nullable and are covered via other tables where present."}, "time": 11.044164600083604}, "1": {"response": {"integrated_schemas": {"Character": {"integrated_schema": ["character_name", "character_description", "character_creator"]}, "Work": {"integrated_schema": ["work_title", "work_description", "work_medium", "work_genre", "release_date"]}, "Person": {"integrated_schema": ["person_name", "person_bio", "birth_date",