yal": "character_name", "CharacterAppearance": "character_name"}, "character_summary": {"Character": "character_description"}, "character_creator": {"Character": "character_creator"}, "portrayed_by": {"Person": "person_name", "Portrayal": "person_name"}, "performer_bio": {"Person": "person_bio"}, "performer_gender": {"Person": "gender"}, "franchise_or_work": {"Work": "work_title", "CharacterAppearance": "work_title", "Portrayal": "work_title"}, "work_summary": {"Work": "work_description"}, "work_format": {"Work": "work_medium"}, "work_genre": {"Work": "work_genre"}, "release_date": {"Work": "release_date"}}}, "reasoning": "Identified recurring real-world entities across all tables: (1) fictional Character, (2) creative Work/franchise the character belongs to, and (3) real Person (actor/performer). To represent relationships without redundancy, two associative entities are used: CharacterAppearance links characters to their source works (needed even when no actor is provided, e.g., Table 3), and Portrayal links a character and a work to the person portraying them (needed where portrayed_by/actor is present). Attributes were unified by synonym mapping (e.g., description/summary/blurb -> character_description; work/source_work/franchise_or_work/title_of_work -> work_title; type/work_format/work_medium -> work_medium; publication_date/first_published_at/release_date -> release_date). Person details were normalized (bio, birth_date, birth_place, gender) and only included where present. This yields a minimal, redundancy-free schema that still covers every source attribute semantically."}, "time": 12.191979499999434}, "2": {"response": {"integrated_schemas": {"Character": {"integrated_schema": ["character_name", "character_description", "creator_name"]}, "Work": {"integrated_schema": ["work_title", "work_description", "medium_format", "genre", "release_publication_date"]}, "Performer": {"integrated_schema": ["performer_name", "performer_bio", "birth_date", "birth_place", "